{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"error-handling","title":"Error Handling & Custom Errors","group":"Advanced Concepts","order":48,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Throw <code>Error</code> objects (never strings), catch where you can actually do something about it, and give your own failures meaningful classes.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield\"></i>try / catch / finally</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function parseAge(input) {\n  const n = Number(input);\n  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n &lt; 0) throw new RangeError(`invalid age: ${input}`);\n  return n;\n}\nfor (const input of [\"42\", \"-3\", \"abc\"]) {\n  try {\n    console.log(\"age\", parseAge(input));\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err.name, \"-\", err.message);\n  } finally {\n    console.log(\"  checked\", JSON.stringify(input));\n  }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>age 42\n  checked \"42\"\nRangeError - invalid age: -3\n  checked \"-3\"\nRangeError - invalid age: abc\n  checked \"abc\"</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Built-in error types</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Thrown when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>SyntaxError</code></td><td>Code (or <code>JSON.parse</code> input) cannot be parsed</td></tr><tr><td><code>ReferenceError</code></td><td>An undeclared variable, or a TDZ access</td></tr><tr><td><code>TypeError</code></td><td>A value is the wrong type: calling a non-function, reading a property of <code>undefined</code>, writing a read-only property</td></tr><tr><td><code>RangeError</code></td><td>A number out of range: <code>new Array(-1)</code>, <code>toFixed(200)</code>, stack overflow</td></tr><tr><td><code>URIError</code></td><td><code>decodeURIComponent('%')</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>AggregateError</code></td><td>Several errors at once (<code>Promise.any</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>EvalError</code></td><td>Legacy, practically unused</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const attempts = [\n  () =&gt; null.name,\n  () =&gt; undefinedVariable,\n  () =&gt; (1).toFixed(500),\n  () =&gt; decodeURIComponent(\"%\"),\n  () =&gt; JSON.parse(\"{bad json}\"),\n];\nfor (const fn of attempts) {\n  try { fn(); } catch (e) { console.log(`${e.name}: ${e.message}`); }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'name')\nReferenceError: undefinedVariable is not defined\nRangeError: toFixed() digits argument must be between 0 and 100\nURIError: URI malformed\nSyntaxError: Expected property name or '}' in JSON at position 1 (line 1 column 2)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Custom error classes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class AppError extends Error {\n  constructor(message, options) {\n    super(message, options);            // options.cause (ES2022)\n    this.name = this.constructor.name;\n  }\n}\nclass NotFoundError extends AppError {\n  constructor(resource, id) {\n    super(`${resource} ${id} not found`);\n    this.status = 404;\n  }\n}\nclass DatabaseError extends AppError {}\n\nfunction findUser(id) {\n  try {\n    throw new Error(\"connection reset\");        // low-level failure\n  } catch (cause) {\n    throw new DatabaseError(`could not load user ${id}`, { cause });\n  }\n}\ntry {\n  findUser(7);\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.name, \"|\", e.message, \"|\", e.cause.message);\n  console.log(e instanceof DatabaseError, e instanceof AppError, e instanceof Error);\n}\nconst nf = new NotFoundError(\"Order\", 42);\nconsole.log(nf.name, nf.status, nf.message);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>DatabaseError | could not load user 7 | connection reset\ntrue true true\nNotFoundError 404 Order 42 not found</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Wrap low-level errors with <code>{ cause }</code> so logs keep the whole story; branch on <code>instanceof</code> (or an error <code>code</code>), not on message text.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bomb\"></i>Uncaught errors</h3>\n<p>An exception nobody catches ends the script (in Node the process exits with code 1). The build confirms this example really fails:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function divide(a, b) {\n  if (b === 0) throw new Error(\"Division by zero\");\n  return a / b;\n}\nconsole.log(divide(10, 2));\nconsole.log(divide(1, 0));   // uncaught → crash\nconsole.log(\"never reached\");</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal (stack trimmed) — exit code 1</figcaption><pre><code>5\nfile:///…/main.mjs:2\n  if (b === 0) throw new Error(\"Division by zero\");\n                     ^\n\nError: Division by zero\n    at divide (file:///…/main.mjs:2:22)\n    at file:///…/main.mjs:6:13\n\nNode.js v24.12.0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock-exclamation\"></i>Async errors</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>try {\n  setTimeout(() =&gt; { /* throw here would NOT be caught below */ }, 0);\n} catch {\n  console.log(\"never\");\n}\nasync function load() { throw new Error(\"async failure\"); }\ntry {\n  await load();                              // await makes try/catch work\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(\"caught:\", e.message);\n}\nload().catch((e) =&gt; console.log(\"caught via .catch:\", e.message));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>caught: async failure\ncaught via .catch: async failure</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Safety net</th><th>Environment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>window.addEventListener('error', …)</code></td><td>Browser: uncaught sync errors</td></tr><tr><td><code>window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', …)</code></td><td>Browser: rejected promises without a handler</td></tr><tr><td><code>process.on('uncaughtException')</code> / <code>('unhandledRejection')</code></td><td>Node: log and exit — don't keep running in an unknown state</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Throw <code>Error</code> instances (they capture a stack trace); catch narrowly and rethrow what you can't handle; always handle promise rejections.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p><code>throw \"oops\"</code> (no stack), empty <code>catch {}</code> blocks that swallow failures, and using exceptions for ordinary control flow.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can try/catch catch errors thrown inside a setTimeout callback?</summary><div><p>No. By the time the callback runs, the <code>try</code> block has long finished — it is a different call stack. Put the <code>try/catch</code> inside the callback, or use promises with <code>await</code>/<code>.catch</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does finally do if both try and finally return?</summary><div><p><code>finally</code> always runs; if it has its own <code>return</code> (or <code>throw</code>), that overrides the result of <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>. Avoid returning from <code>finally</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}